Apps Script deployment and security notes
Deploying the validation endpoint
Open Google Apps Script and create a project.
Copy `code/Code.gs` into the script editor.
Replace `REPLACE_WITH_A_NEW_RANDOM_SECRET` with a newly generated random secret.
Save the project.
Select Deploy → New deployment → Web app.
Set Execute as to your account.
Choose an access setting that allows Make.com to call the endpoint. If using anonymous/public access, understand that anyone who obtains the URL can reach the web app.
Deploy and copy the web-app URL ending in `/exec`.
Configure Make.com's HTTP module with that URL and the same secret in the JSON body.
When changing the script, create a new deployment version using Deploy → Manage deployments → Edit → New version → Deploy.
Secret handling
The repository intentionally contains a placeholder only.
Do not commit the real API secret, even in an example JSON file.
Do not publish screenshots showing the secret.
If a secret has been exposed in a chat, screenshot, log, or repository, rotate it in both Apps Script and Make.com.
Do not rely on a hard-coded shared secret as a complete production authentication system.
Privacy and production considerations
Use synthetic lead data in screenshots and repository examples.
Restrict access to the spreadsheet, Airtable base, Gmail account, and Apps Script project.
Review Apps Script quotas and Google account policies before relying on this for ongoing business use.
Consider stronger authentication, rate limiting, spam protection, consent capture, retention controls, and audit logging before processing real customer information.
The sample script intentionally returns a generic error for unexpected processing failures rather than exposing internal exception details.
